<SolutionPage
  title="Chronic Pain Management"
  parent="Muscle"
  oneliner="Chronic pain is rarely just structural — it's a system-level signal that requires a system-level answer."
  slug="pain-chronic"
>
  <h2>What this is</h2>
  <p>
    Pain that persists beyond 12 weeks has typically stopped being a tissue-damage signal
    and has become a nervous-system pattern. That distinction changes everything about how
    it should be addressed. Most chronic pain management programs focus on symptom suppression;
    the 4M framework focuses on the upstream drivers: inflammation, movement capacity,
    sleep quality, and central sensitization.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is one of the most personally motivating areas for Dr. Mundheim — 30 years of
    clinical practice have shown him that the men who resolve chronic pain are the ones
    who treat it as a whole-system problem, not a structural defect to be fixed once.
  </p>

  <h2>Our approach</h2>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Pain Inventory</h3>
      <p>Map onset, patterns, aggravating factors, and prior interventions — most chronic pain has a story that guides the solution.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Inflammatory Reduction</h3>
      <p>Gut health (Biome-AF), diet quality, sleep, and movement address the most common systemic amplifiers.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Graded Movement Exposure</h3>
      <p>Fear-avoidance is a major perpetuator of chronic pain. Structured progressive loading breaks the cycle.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Mind-Body Integration</h3>
      <p>The Mind pillar (stress regulation, sleep) is not separate from pain management — it's central to it.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</SolutionPage>
